eStack: Mini Manual
-------------------

A practical introduction to using eStack as a layout container in Enigma2 skin XML.


1. What is eStack?
------------------

eStack is a container used to group child widgets and arrange them as a layout. It is useful when several elements
belong together and should be laid out as a unit, rather than each element being positioned independently.
An eStack can contain ordinary widgets, such as labels, and can also contain another eStack. Nesting containers lets
one part of a screen use a different arrangement from another.


2. The basic pattern
--------------------

A typical eStack has a position, a size, a layout direction, optional spacing, and child widgets:

<eStack position="20,20" size="e-40,e-40" layout="vertical" spacing="10">
	<eLabel size="e,50" text="First item" />
	<eLabel size="e,50" text="Second item" />
</eStack>

The attributes:

- position — specifies where the container is placed relative to its parent, using the skin system's position syntax.

- size — specifies the container's width and height using the skin system's size syntax. Expressions such as e-40
  depend on that syntax; they are not ordinary numeric dimensions.
  
- layout — chooses the direction in which the container lays out its children. vertical means a column; horizontal means a row.

- spacing — sets the gap between children. The skin parser passes this value to the stack's spacing setter as an integer.


3. Choosing a layout direction
------------------------------

Use layout="vertical" when items should follow one another down the screen. Use layout="horizontal"
when items should follow one another across the screen.

<!-- A column -->
<eStack layout="vertical" spacing="8" size="e,120">
	<eLabel size="e,50" text="Top item" />
	<eLabel size="e,50" text="Next item" />
</eStack>

<!-- A row -->
<eStack layout="horizontal" spacing="8" size="e,60">
	<eLabel size="120,e" text="First" />
	<eLabel size="120,e" text="Second" />
</eStack>

The examples show the intended grouping and direction. Exact sizing and placement still depend on the Enigma2 skin size/position
rules and the implementation in the version being used.


4. Nesting stacks
-----------------

Nesting is the main way to combine directions. For example, a vertical outer stack can place a header, a horizontal
row of controls, and a footer one after another. The inner horizontal stack handles the controls as a group.

<eStack position="20,20" size="e-40,e-40" layout="vertical" spacing="10">
	<eLabel size="e,50" text="Header" />
	<eStack size="e,70" layout="horizontal" spacing="10">
		<eLabel size="120,e" text="Choice 1" />
		<eLabel size="120,e" text="Choice 2" />
		<eLabel size="120,e" text="Choice 3" />
	</eStack>
	<eLabel size="e,40" text="Footer" />
</eStack>


5. Understanding child positions and sizes
------------------------------------------

A child widget may have its own position and size attributes. Values such as top, bottom, left, and right
are skin-system placement values; they should not be assumed to behave like ordinary pixel coordinates.
The exact rules for how those placement values interact with eStack's layout direction, and how multiple children with
the same placement value are ordered or sized, are implementation-specific. If a layout relies on that detail, verify it
against the relevant Enigma2 implementation or test it in the image.


6. Practical workflow
---------------------

- Start with one eStack and two simple children so the direction and spacing are easy to see.
- Set the container's size first; then choose child sizes that make sense within it.
- Use a nested eStack when a group of children needs a different direction from the outer group.
- Add spacing gradually and check the rendered result for gaps or overflow.
- Test on the Enigma2 version and skin environment where the XML will run.


7. Quick reference
------------------

Need a column? Use layout="vertical".
Need a row? Use layout="horizontal".
Need both? Nest one eStack inside another.
Need gaps? Set spacing.
Need exact placement behavior? Check the Enigma2 implementation or test the skin; do not infer all placement rules from the XML syntax alone.